meta {
  name: Mark Primary Contact Deceased (Succession Required)
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/contacts/{{householdMemberContactId}}
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Authorization: Bearer {{clientAdminToken}}
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "deceasedAt": "2026-02-14"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 409 Conflict", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(409);
  });

  test("asks who takes over, by code", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.code).to.equal("primary_contact_succession_required");
    expect(body.householdId).to.equal(bru.getVar("householdId"));
    expect(body.contactId).to.equal(bru.getVar("householdMemberContactId"));
  });

  test("offers the household's other living members as candidates", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.candidates).to.be.an("array");
    // Never the person dying, and never anyone who already leads another
    // household — the exclusivity rule applies to the promotion too.
    for (const candidate of body.candidates) {
      expect(candidate.id).to.not.equal(bru.getVar("householdMemberContactId"));
      expect(candidate).to.have.property("name");
      expect(candidate).to.have.property("role");
    }
  });

  test("writes nothing — the refusal leaves the contact alive", function () {
    // Succession is settled BEFORE the contact is saved, so a refusal cannot
    // leave a household led by somebody already recorded as dead.
    expect(res.getBody()).to.not.have.property("deceasedAt");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /contacts/:id — marking a household's primary contact deceased

  The refusal half of PAC-91 §7's succession rule. Runs against the member
  `Set Primary Contact` just promoted, so the target is **provably** a
  household's primary contact on any database — and the request writes nothing,
  so it is safe to re-run.

  - **Module:** `clients`. **Permission:** `clients:write`.
  - Sent as the **agency owner** (`Login as Client Manager`), not the
    collection's producer: `PATCH /contacts/:id` derives an `own`-scope caller's
    reach from the leads that touch the contact, and a member added through the
    "+ Member" dialog has none.

  ## Why one edit needs two answers

  A contact can die (David, 2026-09-04), and when the deceased was a household's
  primary contact another member has to take over. Recording the death alone
  would leave the household headed by somebody who is dead — so the same request
  has to answer who leads it now. The owner's rule, written down: **(a) name the
  successor in the same operation**, falling back to **(c) a deliberate, flagged
  gap**.

  Auto-promotion by role precedence (Spouse -> adult member -> ...) was
  considered and rejected. It guesses at something the producer knows, on the
  record that decides which policies and which producer the whole family hangs
  off.

  ## Body
  | Field | Notes |
  |---|---|
  | `deceasedAt` | `YYYY-MM-DD`, or `null` to undo. A future date is a **400** |
  | `successorContactId` | Rule (a). Held to the same three rules as `POST /households/:id/primary-contact` |
  | `allowNoPrimary` | Rule (c). Clears the primary and sets `dataQuality: "no_primary"` |

  The last two are only meaningful alongside a `deceasedAt` that is being set;
  sent alone they are a **400** rather than a silent no-op.

  ## Response — 409 `primary_contact_succession_required`

  ```json
  {
    "statusCode": 409,
    "code": "primary_contact_succession_required",
    "contactId": "...",
    "householdId": "...",
    "householdRef": "HH-2614",
    "householdName": "Tyson Household",
    "candidates": [{ "id": "...", "name": "Sasha Successor", "role": "Spouse" }]
  }
  ```

  `candidates` is every current member who could take over: alive, and not
  already the primary of another household. It can be **empty** and that is not
  an error — a household whose only member has died has nobody to promote, and
  the caller's next step is `allowNoPrimary`.

  Nothing is written. Succession is settled *before* the contact is saved, so
  the only reachable partial state is a household with a new primary whose old
  one is not yet marked — merely out of date. The other order would leave a
  household led by a dead person, which is the state §7 exists to make
  unreachable.
}
